<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayyida Shirin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1028</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayyida Shirin was a Buyid queen who ruled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as regent for her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> from 997 until her death in 1028. A princess of the Bavandid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, she kept power by skilful diplomacy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> is said to have held back from attacking while she lived. He took Rayy in 1029, the year after her death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sayyida Shirin was the daughter of Rustam II (r. 964–979), the ispahbadh of the Bavand dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, a family that traced its descent to Bav, alleged grandson of the Sasanian prince Kawus, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a>. She married the Buyid amir Fakhr al-Dawla, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, Tabaristan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a>, and bore him two sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla">Shams al-Dawla</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Fakhr al-Dawla died of a stomach illness in 997, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> was divided: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> received Ray and its surroundings, and his younger brother, who remained his subordinate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and Qirmisin. Sayyida Shirin installed both and ruled as regent. In these years the Ziyarid Qabus retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a> and the Sallarids took Zanjan and other towns, while the Hasanwayhid chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-ibn-hasanwayh">Badr ibn Hasanwayh</a>, his help rebuffed, drew away from Ray. By 1003 at the latest she had made her first cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, the start of the Kakuyid dynasty.</p><p>In 1008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> tried to end her regency. She fled to Badr, besieged Ray with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla">Shams al-Dawla</a>, captured her son and imprisoned him in the fort of Tabarak; a year later she fell out with Shams al-Dawla and restored Majd al-Dawla, keeping power herself. In 1014 an attack by Shams al-Dawla drove them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damavand">Damavand</a> until his troops mutinied. That year the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> entered their service as court physician, treating Majd al-Dawla's melancholia, and reportedly managed her affairs in Qazvin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>. In 1016 the Daylamite officer Ibn Fuladh, refused Qazvin, besieged Ray with 2,000 troops from the Ziyarid Manuchihr and was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She died in 1028, and her son's isolation soon showed. Facing a revolt of his Daylamite soldiers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> asked the Ghaznavid ruler Mahmud for help. The historian Clifford Edmund Bosworth calls this a foolish decision, since Mahmud had long wished to expand westward but had held back from Ray because of her resolute presence. Using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>' Shi'a beliefs as a pretext, Mahmud came to Ray in 1029, deposed Majd al-Dawla and sacked the city, ending Buyid rule there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sayyida Shirin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ala al-Dawla Muhammad, called Ibn Kakuya because he was the maternal uncle (kaku) of the Buyid regent <b>Sayyida</b>, was appointed governor of Isfahan around 1008.</blockquote><a href="/state/kakuyid-dynasty">Kakuyid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Ray, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36,51&amp;year=1018" aria-label="Tabaristan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5836 864 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sayyida Shirin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6286 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6286 1134h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36,51&amp;year=1018">What was here in 1018? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
